  "query_preview": "..A shortwave lifting northeastward ahead of the large\nwest coast trough is focusing convection in northwest Arizona up\ninto Utah early this afternoon. This area also started the day\nsunny, so additional instability was able to build. This is where\nthe highest flash flood threat will be (north/west Coconino\nCounty) into this evening, with WPC upgrading the area to a\nmoderate risk for flash flooding. Elsewhere, the large area of\nshowers and thunderstorms that moved northward through much of the\nregion left overnight behind a cooler and more stable boundary \nlayer with surface temperatures mostly in the 60s. So the threat \nof stronger storms outside of north/west Coconino County has \nlowered. However, with deep moisture in place and some \nredevelopment still possible, will leave the Flash Flood Watch in \nplace into this evening. Tuesday through Thursday, southwest flow\naloft will increase as the western US trough moves closer, \nbringing some drier air into our region but not completely\nscouring out the low level moisture. As a result, there is still a\nchance for some isolated showers and thunderstorms each afternoon,\nbut intensity appears to be less than what we have seen the last\nseveral days. Daytime high temperatures will be a few degrees\nbelow normal through the period. Southwest breezes will also\nincrease due to the trough, reaching 15-20 mph and gusting up to\n30 mph at times.\n\nFriday into the weekend - high pressure nudges back westward as\nthe west coast trough retreats a bit. This should allow for a more\nsoutherly flow with moisture returning to the area. The timing of\nthe moisture return, and the influence of a possible tropical\nsystem off the coast of Baja remain uncertain. This will impact\njust how much moisture we end up seeing. For now, storm chances\nrise to the 30-60% range for Friday through Sunday.",
